# Performance

Hublee ships large bundled assets (the KFGQPC mushaf JSON alone is ~4.3 MB) and renders
complex Arabic text. Both make it easy to write code that is correct but janky. These
rules exist because each one has already been a real problem in this codebase.

## Never create futures in `build()`

`FutureBuilder(future: repository.load(...))` inside `build()` re-fetches on every
rebuild and flickers the UI. Cache the future in `initState()`:

```dart
late final Future<Surah> _surahFuture;

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  _surahFuture = const QuranArabicRepository().loadArabicSurah(widget.surahId);
}
```

## Cache parsed assets

Decoding a multi-megabyte JSON file is expensive and happens on the main isolate.

- Parse each asset **once per session** and hold the result in a static cache in the
  repository, keyed by whatever varies (surah id, book file).
- Never decode the full mushaf to read a single ayah. Prefer the per-surah files
  (`assets/quran/ar/<surahId>.json`) when you only need one surah.
- For genuinely heavy parsing or search, move the work off the main isolate with
  `compute()`.

## Keep long lists lazy

- Use `ListView.builder` / `ScrollablePositionedList.builder` — never a `ListView` with
  a pre-built `children:` list for the 114 surahs, the full book list, or any ayah list.
- Anything wrapped in `SingleChildScrollView` + `Column` builds every child up front.
- Add `RepaintBoundary` around list items that paint expensive content (tajweed-coloured
  Arabic) so scrolling doesn't repaint neighbours.

## No expensive work inside `itemBuilder`

`itemBuilder` runs on every scroll frame for every visible item. Inside it, avoid:

- **O(n) lookups.** `bookmarks.any((b) => b.id == id)` per row is O(n²) overall. Back
  membership checks with a `Set<String>` or `Map`.
- **Repeated filtering.** Don't filter a list to find "the items in this juz" per row —
  group once, ahead of the build.
- **String matching or parsing.** Precompute summaries and titles once.
- **Re-running tajweed analysis.** `tajweedSpans()` walks the string and does per-letter
  rule detection; memoize its output per (text, brightness, style) rather than
  recomputing on every rebuild.

## Debounce persistence

`SharedPreferences` writes hit disk. A `ChangeNotifier` setter that writes and calls
`notifyListeners()` on every slider tick causes a write and a full-subtree rebuild per
frame while dragging.

- Notify immediately for responsive UI, but **debounce the write** (~300 ms).
- Don't rewrite an entire JSON blob for a single-item change when an incremental
  update is possible.

## Keep scope rebuilds narrow

`SettingsScope` and `BookmarkScope` are `InheritedNotifier`s near the root, so a
`notifyListeners()` can rebuild most of the tree.

- Implement `updateShouldNotify` to compare only the fields that matter.
- Have widgets depend on the narrowest scope they need; don't read `SettingsScope` in a
  widget that only cares about bookmarks.
- A toggle that affects one row should not rebuild the whole page.

## Startup

- Don't block first paint on work the first screen doesn't need.
- Load persisted settings **before** the first frame that depends on them, or the app
  visibly flashes from the default theme to the user's theme.

## Verifying

Measure before and after — don't assume. Use the Dart/Flutter MCP tools
(`get_runtime_errors`, `widget_inspector`) and Flutter DevTools' timeline. A change is
only a performance fix if you can point at a frame time or a removed decode.
